course: dictionary
lesson: 7187
title: Persuade: getting someone to agree
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To persuade someone is to make them agree to do something or believe something. You can do this by giving good reasons or by asking nicely. «Shouting does not persuade anyone; reasons and evidence do.» Shouting may make people cross, but reasons make them think.
explain: Persuade can also mean to convince someone that something is true. «The serpent persuaded Eve that God was keeping something good from her.» Here persuaded is followed by that, and the thing that is believed comes after it.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Who is doing the persuading, and what do they want? In «A clever serpent persuades Eve that God is keeping something good from her», the serpent is trying to change what Eve believes. The word clever is a clue that the serpent is good at it.
explain: Liam in Leeds mutters "Nah, that's mardy" about the other side. Mardy means grumpy or sulky, so a grumble does not persuade anyone. His teacher asks him to explain why he disagrees, because a reason can persuade. Good writers persuade with reasons, facts and strong words.
fact: Persuade is a verb: persuade, persuades, persuaded, persuading.
fact: «Persuade someone to do something» means to get them to agree to act.
fact: «Persuade someone that» means to convince them that something is true.
fact: «Persuasive writing» uses reasons, facts and strong words to persuade the reader.
fact: «Be persuaded» means to be made to agree or believe something.
exercise: «The serpent persuaded Eve that God was keeping something good from her.» What does persuaded mean here? | Made her laugh loudly | Made her forget the fruit | Made her run away | *Made her believe something
exercise: Which is the best way to persuade someone to let you have a pet? | *Give good reasons, such as how you will look after it | Stop talking to them | Hide the pet in your bedroom | Shout until they give in
exercise: Which word in «Shouting does not persuade anyone; reasons and evidence do.» names something that can persuade? | shouting | does | anyone | *reasons
test: Liam says a classmate's idea is "mardy". What does mardy mean? | Happy and excited | Lost and confused | *Grumpy or sulky | Clever and quick
test: In «persuasive writing», what do writers use to persuade the reader? | *Reasons, facts and strong words | Questions they cannot answer | Pictures with no words | Only long words
exam: Which sentence uses persuade to mean convince someone that something is true? | *She persuaded her brother that the ghost story was made up | She persuaded her cousin to come to the park | He ran fast and persuaded the bus to wait | They persuaded the rain to stop
